<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Thomond</h1><p class="st-meta">1118 CE – 1543 CE · capital Limerick; later Clonroad (Ennis)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The O'Brien kingdom of North Munster, descendant of Brian Boru's power base, which kept its independence until 1543.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Thomond arose from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-cais">Dal Cais</a> lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-clare">Clare</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a>. In 1118 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-glanmire">Treaty of Glanmire</a>, imposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toirdelbach-ua-conchobair">Turlough O'Connor</a>, split <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Munster</a>, leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obrien-dynasty">O'Briens</a> with the north, Tuadmumu. Their capital was Limerick, the former <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-limerick">Kingdom of Limerick</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obrien-dynasty">O'Brien</a> kings were patrons of churches such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-cross-abbey">Holy Cross</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/killaloe">Killaloe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick's</a> cathedral, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domnall-mor-ua-briain">Domnall Mor O'Brien</a>. After losing Limerick to the Normans in the 1190s, they ruled from Clonroad near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ennis">Ennis</a>. In 1318 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muirchertach-o-briain-d-1343">Muirchertach O'Brien</a> defeated the Normans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dysert-odea">Dysert O'Dea</a>, preserving Gaelic rule west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-shannon">Shannon</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1543 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murrough-obrien-1st-earl-of-thomond">Murrough O'Brien</a> surrendered his kingship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> and became Earl of Thomond under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland</a>. His lands became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-clare">County Clare</a> in the 1570s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-thomond.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Thomond" width="320" height="184"><figcaption>Image: self-created · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Thomond.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1118 CE – 1543 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Limerick; later Clonroad (Ennis)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Domnall Mor O'Brien, Muirchertach O'Brien, Murrough O'Brien</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tuadmumu, North Munster</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1330&amp;focus=kingdom-of-thomond" aria-label="Kingdom of Thomond on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4479 357 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Thomond lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4799 613L4823 622M4799 613L4861 599M4799 613L4861 599"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4823 622h0M4861 599h0M4861 599h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4799 613h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4799 613h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1330&amp;focus=kingdom-of-thomond">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
